WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:04.340
Speaker 1: It's great to welcome Bob Kuttner
to the program today, who is coeditor of the

00:00:04.340 --> 00:00:10.340
American Prospect magazine, also chair at
the Heller School for Social Policy and Management

00:00:10.340 --> 00:00:11.870
at Brandeis University.

00:00:11.870 --> 00:00:13.440
I really appreciate your time today.

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:14.440
Thanks for being on.

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:15.770
Pleasure to be with you.

00:00:15.770 --> 00:00:20.189
So I've been interested in the idea of economic
nationalism for some time.

00:00:20.189 --> 00:00:25.300
And one of the I guess where we can maybe
start is whether there is some inconsistency

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:30.800
among some on the left on this issue, because
I'm still developing sort of my sense of this.

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:38.750
But there is a sort of global egalitarianism
that I see from many who, like me, describe

00:00:38.750 --> 00:00:41.830
themselves as being on the left and progressives.

00:00:41.830 --> 00:00:47.370
That seems to stop short when it comes to,
well, we should have an economy that prioritizes

00:00:47.370 --> 00:00:48.760
the American worker.

00:00:48.760 --> 00:00:53.410
We should have trade deals that are best for
the United States.

00:00:53.410 --> 00:00:56.650
Is there a conflict or an inconsistency there?

00:00:56.650 --> 00:00:58.450
Speaker 3: Not necessarily.

00:00:58.450 --> 00:01:02.710
And I think it's really important to start
out by defining what we mean.

00:01:02.710 --> 00:01:08.060
And I think economic nationalism means two
different things that sometimes get conflated

00:01:08.060 --> 00:01:09.060
rather sloppily.

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:10.060
So good.

00:01:10.060 --> 00:01:11.590
Let's start with that, please.

00:01:11.590 --> 00:01:18.229
So if you are a Roosevelt progressive and
you're not a neo liberal and you think that

00:01:18.229 --> 00:01:24.969
capitalism is predatory, and in order to have
a system that is efficient and socially just,

00:01:24.969 --> 00:01:27.810
the government has to be involved big time.

00:01:27.810 --> 00:01:33.569
A democratic government has to be involved
big time in regulating capitalism.

00:01:33.569 --> 00:01:40.639
That means that you have to put limits on
globalism, because if you have what we've

00:01:40.639 --> 00:01:47.799
had for the past 30 or 40 years with the WTO
and the use of trade deals to undercut the

00:01:47.799 --> 00:01:50.500
regulation of capitalism, you can't regulate
capitalism.

00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:58.119
I mean, banks can go offshore and we don't
care whether products that we import are made

00:01:58.119 --> 00:01:59.159
with slave labor.

00:01:59.159 --> 00:02:01.429
You know, the whole litany.

00:02:01.429 --> 00:02:02.429
Sure.

00:02:02.429 --> 00:02:06.659
Economic nationalism, in one sense of the
word simply means that we have to regulate

00:02:06.659 --> 00:02:08.039
capitalism.

00:02:08.039 --> 00:02:10.960
And we, for better or worse, is the nation
state.

00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:12.160
There's no global economy.

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:16.810
You can't regulate capitalism with a republic
of naphtha.

00:02:16.810 --> 00:02:20.910
You have to regulate it with the United States
of America, where where I'm a citizen and

00:02:20.910 --> 00:02:21.910
you're a citizen.

00:02:21.910 --> 00:02:27.480
Speaker 1: And to some degree, is that also
just the practicality of legal framework and

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:30.740
just sort of the limits of what any one group
can do?

00:02:30.740 --> 00:02:32.370
I mean, I know we have we have NATO.

00:02:32.370 --> 00:02:35.520
We have you know, we have these organizations,
but it doesn't get sloppy.

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:37.540
Speaker 3: Well, it's about sovereignty.

00:02:37.540 --> 00:02:38.540
Yeah.

00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:41.800
If you want to regulate capitalism, you need
a degree of sovereignty.

00:02:41.800 --> 00:02:48.390
And the state is sovereign and there is no
sovereignty globally except to the extent

00:02:48.390 --> 00:02:49.620
that you have treaties.

00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:50.620
Right.

00:02:50.620 --> 00:02:55.390
The other kind of economic nationalism of
which Trump is the extreme example or maybe

00:02:55.390 --> 00:03:00.930
Hitler's the more extreme example is America
first or Germany first and screw the rest

00:03:00.930 --> 00:03:02.130
of the world.

00:03:02.130 --> 00:03:08.830
And so where it gets tricky is on the question
of how you make it possible for the whole

00:03:08.830 --> 00:03:14.010
world to regulate capitalism in the interest
of ordinary people.

00:03:14.010 --> 00:03:18.071
And since we don't have a global government,
that means you have to have agreements, you

00:03:18.071 --> 00:03:19.071
have to have treaties.

00:03:19.071 --> 00:03:25.170
This morning's New York Times reports that
the E.U. is going to have climate tariffs.

00:03:25.170 --> 00:03:33.470
If you if you try to export stuff into the
EU that are made with processes and products

00:03:33.470 --> 00:03:36.230
that pollute, you're going to have to pay
a tariff.

00:03:36.230 --> 00:03:39.620
Now, that's a good kind of nationalism.

00:03:39.620 --> 00:03:43.290
And Biden is talking about doing the same
thing.

00:03:43.290 --> 00:03:49.530
So the tricky part for me, anyway, because
I'm you know, I'm a third world guy as well

00:03:49.530 --> 00:03:51.600
as a new dealer.

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:56.670
How do you extend this so that the whole world
is able to regulate predatory capitalism?

00:03:56.670 --> 00:04:01.600
And interestingly, that is what was attempted
in nineteen forty four at the Bretton Woods

00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:06.590
conference where the architect of that conference
with John Maynard Keynes, he got a do over,

00:04:06.590 --> 00:04:11.710
you know, they got it wrong with the Treaty
of Versailles in in nineteen nineteen, where

00:04:11.710 --> 00:04:14.100
he was a junior economic adviser to the British
government.

00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:17.420
He quit in disgust, in protest.

00:04:17.420 --> 00:04:20.489
And then twenty five years later, he was the
chair of the Bretton Woods conference.

00:04:20.489 --> 00:04:27.310
And he said we have to orchestrate the rules
of the global system so that every country

00:04:27.310 --> 00:04:32.620
gets to regulate capitalism in the interests
of workers, in the interests of citizens.

00:04:32.620 --> 00:04:34.910
Now, here's the tricky part.

00:04:34.910 --> 00:04:37.030
You get down to cases.

00:04:37.030 --> 00:04:42.950
So if we think that China is predatory, we
we think that China violates human rights.

00:04:42.950 --> 00:04:47.680
We think that China violates the rules of
the trading system where they subsidize and

00:04:47.680 --> 00:04:53.310
they dump and they steal intellectual property
and all that stuff.

00:04:53.310 --> 00:04:59.150
And so we're going to have some we're going
to have an industrial policy and we're going

00:04:59.150 --> 00:05:00.150
to have a.

00:05:00.150 --> 00:05:05.880
National security policy, where certain technologies
we're not going to allow China to acquire.

00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:08.180
That seems pretty sensible to have an industrial
policy.

00:05:08.180 --> 00:05:10.000
Where do you draw the line?

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:15.070
And is that an industrial policy for the United
States or is that an industrial policy that

00:05:15.070 --> 00:05:18.860
the EU and all of the democracies are part
of?

00:05:18.860 --> 00:05:23.780
And then if that's the case, who gets the
business?

00:05:23.780 --> 00:05:31.080
So, for instance, there are three major chip
companies that are not Chinese that make state

00:05:31.080 --> 00:05:33.590
of the art chips once Intel.

00:05:33.590 --> 00:05:36.690
The other two are European, Ericsson and Nokia.

00:05:36.690 --> 00:05:37.750
So do you have a cartel?

00:05:37.750 --> 00:05:43.700
Do you carve up the market or do you say we
have one set of rules for companies that play

00:05:43.700 --> 00:05:50.580
by democratic norms and market norms and a
different set of rules from China, which doesn't

00:05:50.580 --> 00:05:52.080
play by market norms.

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:58.660
So where it gets tricky is around the edges
where when you get down to cases and here's

00:05:58.660 --> 00:06:07.980
Vietnam, third world country, a dictatorship,
but not as nasty a dictatorship as China.

00:06:07.980 --> 00:06:13.860
And a lot of stuff gets outsourced to Vietnam
by American companies.

00:06:13.860 --> 00:06:16.750
And these are very poor people who need the
work.

00:06:16.750 --> 00:06:18.830
This is where it gets tricky.

00:06:18.830 --> 00:06:23.620
And so you have to have trade agreements that
are very different from the kind of trade

00:06:23.620 --> 00:06:29.440
agreements that Clinton and Obama made that
have human rights in them, that have some

00:06:29.440 --> 00:06:37.150
concern for the ability of Third World countries
to get a foot on the ladder so that they can

00:06:37.150 --> 00:06:43.130
have decent living standards for their for
their people, but not simply turning the world

00:06:43.130 --> 00:06:44.310
over to multinational corporations.

00:06:44.310 --> 00:06:45.510
Speaker 1: Yes.

00:06:45.510 --> 00:06:51.580
And I wanted to I mean, let's talk a little
bit about trade, if we can, because the the

00:06:51.580 --> 00:06:57.210
the concept of free trade being a net positive
is rooted in some pretty traditional economic

00:06:57.210 --> 00:07:04.220
ideas of specialization and relative efficiencies
of producing and exporting products.

00:07:04.220 --> 00:07:09.389
And it really goes back to a lot of that that
traditional economics.

00:07:09.389 --> 00:07:16.660
And at the end of the day, as you're pointing
out, sometimes the best way to control conditions

00:07:16.660 --> 00:07:23.840
when it comes to labor and products and imports
is you bring you bring the supply lines domestic.

00:07:23.840 --> 00:07:28.560
But at the same time, then you're depriving,
as you point out, a lot of people around the

00:07:28.560 --> 00:07:35.160
world of the ability to better themselves
and better, better their economic situation.

00:07:35.160 --> 00:07:38.630
So how how would how should we interpret all
of that and sort of parse it?

00:07:38.630 --> 00:07:39.740
Speaker 3: Yeah.

00:07:39.740 --> 00:07:42.780
Let me address what you said about trade being
efficient.

00:07:42.780 --> 00:07:44.670
And then let me respond to the second question.

00:07:44.670 --> 00:07:46.580
Yeah, well, here's a conundrum.

00:07:46.580 --> 00:07:48.530
David, Ricardo, etc..

00:07:48.530 --> 00:07:54.100
Theory of comparative advantage trade is efficient
because of specialization.

00:07:54.100 --> 00:07:56.120
OK, but hold on.

00:07:56.120 --> 00:08:01.680
We have known ever since Roosevelt and before,
really, that free markets are not efficient.

00:08:01.680 --> 00:08:02.680
Right?

00:08:02.680 --> 00:08:04.560
Markets priced things wrong.

00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:08.550
Markets priced carbon wrong.

00:08:08.550 --> 00:08:14.240
Lord Nicholas Stern said global climate change
is history's epic case of market failure.

00:08:14.240 --> 00:08:20.630
Right markets price labor wrong because corporations
have the power to really exploit workers,

00:08:20.630 --> 00:08:23.580
markets, price, all kinds of stuff wrong.

00:08:23.580 --> 00:08:25.700
They priced housing wrong.

00:08:25.700 --> 00:08:31.040
So question, how is it that we need to have
a managed form of capitalism domestically

00:08:31.040 --> 00:08:35.890
because markets priced things wrong and then
somehow when you cross borders, all of a sudden

00:08:35.890 --> 00:08:40.010
markets price things right and the answer
is they don't.

00:08:40.010 --> 00:08:43.860
And this gets into power relationships.

00:08:43.860 --> 00:08:50.860
You want Third World countries to be able
to get jobs and gain market share, but you

00:08:50.860 --> 00:08:52.730
don't want them to exploit their workers.

00:08:52.730 --> 00:08:57.430
So let me give you an example of a trade deal
that I think was a pretty good trade deal.

00:08:57.430 --> 00:09:01.220
Yeah, there used to be something called the
multi fiber agreement.

00:09:01.220 --> 00:09:05.310
It was a worldwide cartel for textiles and
apparel.

00:09:05.310 --> 00:09:12.130
And the idea was that we're going to give
every country that wants to export textile

00:09:12.130 --> 00:09:15.140
products into the United States a quota.

00:09:15.140 --> 00:09:16.510
And the economists that this is terrible.

00:09:16.510 --> 00:09:20.430
This is a cartel that violates the free market.

00:09:20.430 --> 00:09:25.270
And the other side of the argument was, well,
if you if you just eliminate it, China will

00:09:25.270 --> 00:09:30.020
get everything because China will underprice
everybody and all these other countries will

00:09:30.020 --> 00:09:34.441
get the short end of the stick.

00:09:34.441 --> 00:09:35.441
Speaker 1: Common argument, huh?

00:09:35.441 --> 00:09:36.441
A commonly heard, commonly repeated.

00:09:36.441 --> 00:09:37.441
Yes.

00:09:37.441 --> 00:09:40.880
Speaker 3: So what they did for a while, this
was in the last two years of Clinton when

00:09:40.880 --> 00:09:45.390
Bob Rice was secretary of Labor and actually
had some influence.

00:09:45.390 --> 00:09:47.260
They they made an agreement.

00:09:47.260 --> 00:09:49.570
Cambodia was the model country.

00:09:49.570 --> 00:09:57.230
And the agreement was if you follow international
labor organization norms and you give workers

00:09:57.230 --> 00:09:59.610
the right to organize.

00:09:59.610 --> 00:10:09.020
We will give you additional quota slots under
the multi fiber arrangement, and so thanks

00:10:09.020 --> 00:10:16.040
to that, the labor movement got a foothold
in Cambodia and workers actually started being

00:10:16.040 --> 00:10:17.250
paid better.

00:10:17.250 --> 00:10:18.800
It didn't hurt their market share.

00:10:18.800 --> 00:10:19.990
It helped their market share.

00:10:19.990 --> 00:10:23.700
And then, of course, the Republicans come
in and they just blow away the whole thing.

00:10:23.700 --> 00:10:25.170
So there's a middle ground.

00:10:25.170 --> 00:10:26.660
I guess this is my punch line.

00:10:26.660 --> 00:10:32.870
Yeah, there's a middle ground between absolutely
free trade, which is a mirage anyway.

00:10:32.870 --> 00:10:39.390
And Trumpy, an economic nationalism, which
is a kind of negotiated trade where you have

00:10:39.390 --> 00:10:43.870
some norms, where you have some standards
and the standards are not just a code word

00:10:43.870 --> 00:10:45.890
way of screwing the Third World.

00:10:45.890 --> 00:10:50.860
And a lot of the Third World diplomats have
made the argument that the West is hypocritical

00:10:50.860 --> 00:10:52.680
of the West.

00:10:52.680 --> 00:10:56.220
Talks about the International Labor Organization,
talks about labor standards.

00:10:56.220 --> 00:10:58.180
That's just protectionist.

00:10:58.180 --> 00:11:02.390
And again, you've got to get down to cases
depending on the particular rules that are

00:11:02.390 --> 00:11:03.390
negotiated.

00:11:03.390 --> 00:11:07.640
It can be protectionist or it can be in the
interests of workers everywhere.

00:11:07.640 --> 00:11:15.860
So you have the case of the Bangladesh horrible
Bangladesh fires where this is a very poor

00:11:15.860 --> 00:11:17.910
country.

00:11:17.910 --> 00:11:23.390
The job that workers don't die in fires when
you don't do that as a covert way of depressing

00:11:23.390 --> 00:11:25.550
their market share, you've got to get down
to cases.

00:11:25.550 --> 00:11:27.172
I guess that's the point.

00:11:27.172 --> 00:11:32.540
Speaker 1: The last thing I wanted to touch
on briefly relates to the coronavirus pandemic.

00:11:32.540 --> 00:11:39.200
We've talked to economists, social psychologists,
all sorts of different folks about the impacts

00:11:39.200 --> 00:11:44.490
that they see as a result of the coronavirus
pandemic and what we've sort of learned learned

00:11:44.490 --> 00:11:50.620
from it when it comes to an economic nationalism
perspective, globalization, supply chains,

00:11:50.620 --> 00:11:51.620
et cetera.

00:11:51.620 --> 00:11:53.990
What do you see as the big takeaways from
the pandemic?

00:11:53.990 --> 00:11:57.330
Speaker 3: Well, I think and it's not just
a pandemic.

00:11:57.330 --> 00:12:02.770
I mean, the unfortunate thing about the pandemic
is that it moves this center stage and gets

00:12:02.770 --> 00:12:04.459
people talking about it.

00:12:04.459 --> 00:12:16.610
So I think it demonstrated that reliance on
supply chains, a lot of which are in countries

00:12:16.610 --> 00:12:21.930
who are not necessarily our friends, is risky
economically and risky politically.

00:12:21.930 --> 00:12:25.930
The question is, which stuff do we want to
make in the United States?

00:12:25.930 --> 00:12:27.450
For what reasons?

00:12:27.450 --> 00:12:31.940
We want to make it in the United States for
industrial policy and technological leadership

00:12:31.940 --> 00:12:37.350
and jobs reasons we want to make in the United
States for national security reasons.

00:12:37.350 --> 00:12:39.180
And where do we draw the line?

00:12:39.180 --> 00:12:42.709
Take take solar cells.

00:12:42.709 --> 00:12:45.570
America was once the leader in solar solar
panels.

00:12:45.570 --> 00:12:48.620
China basically undercut America's pricing.

00:12:48.620 --> 00:12:51.529
So a lot of technology, they're now the leader.

00:12:51.529 --> 00:12:56.890
If we take it back, try to take it back with
an industrial policy, that means we're paying

00:12:56.890 --> 00:12:59.220
more money in the short run for solar panels.

00:12:59.220 --> 00:13:00.220
Right.

00:13:00.220 --> 00:13:04.160
Which are more expensive if we're trying to
get rid of carbon.

00:13:04.160 --> 00:13:06.330
So it's complicated.

00:13:06.330 --> 00:13:09.550
You cannot settle this at the level of grand
theory.

00:13:09.550 --> 00:13:11.660
You have to have some basic norms.

00:13:11.660 --> 00:13:17.890
We want a positive sum game that helps American
workers and also helps the third world.

00:13:17.890 --> 00:13:18.970
And then you've got to get very specific.

00:13:18.970 --> 00:13:23.670
Speaker 1: Yeah, and often I think that missing
from the conversation where we hear just this

00:13:23.670 --> 00:13:30.180
very vague and general bring the production
back home, there's very little discussion

00:13:30.180 --> 00:13:33.990
of the impact that that would have on the
prices of many of the things that we're talking

00:13:33.990 --> 00:13:37.769
about, as you point out, with solar panels,
which is a decision that could be made.

00:13:37.769 --> 00:13:39.459
I mean, the decision could be made.

00:13:39.459 --> 00:13:43.899
Here's a list of 10 areas where we believe
it's worth paying more.

00:13:43.899 --> 00:13:47.540
Here's how much access would be reduced for
the American people as a result of that.

00:13:47.540 --> 00:13:48.690
But we believe it's worth it.

00:13:48.690 --> 00:13:55.290
Speaker 3: Well, and by the way, this is what
Japan and Korea and China all did at earlier

00:13:55.290 --> 00:13:56.910
stages of their economic development.

00:13:56.910 --> 00:14:01.990
I mean, Ricardi and economists would have
said to the Japanese, why in the world do

00:14:01.990 --> 00:14:05.540
you want to invest in an auto industry or
a steel industry or a machine to an industry

00:14:05.540 --> 00:14:08.300
because you can get it cheaper from the United
States.

00:14:08.300 --> 00:14:12.279
And the Japanese answer was, well, depends
on what your time horizon is if your time

00:14:12.279 --> 00:14:13.730
horizon is this year.

00:14:13.730 --> 00:14:18.490
Yeah, but if your time horizon is 50 years
or 100 years or a thousand years, we want

00:14:18.490 --> 00:14:23.089
that industry in our country because that's
good for our economic growth and for our jobs.

00:14:23.089 --> 00:14:28.060
So you can't just take a snapshot at a point
in time and look at the China price.

00:14:28.060 --> 00:14:31.899
Look at the US price and say, oh, well, obviously
we've got to buy from China.

00:14:31.899 --> 00:14:34.209
So it really it's it's it's tricky.

00:14:34.209 --> 00:14:36.240
It's complicated.

00:14:36.240 --> 00:14:38.800
And you've got to get you've got to get into
the details in the particulars.

00:14:38.800 --> 00:14:40.300
Speaker 1: Without question.

00:14:40.300 --> 00:14:41.300
Without question.

00:14:41.300 --> 00:14:45.839
We've been speaking with Bob Kuttner, who's
coeditor of The American Prospect, also chair

00:14:45.839 --> 00:14:50.180
at the Heller School for Social Policy and
Management at Brandeis University.

00:14:50.180 --> 00:14:52.130
I really appreciate your time today and your
insights.

00:14:52.130 --> 00:14:53.220
Speaker 3: Thanks so much for having me.

